DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ssiiggssuussppeenndd() system call temporarily changes the blocked signal mask
     to the set to which _s_i_g_m_a_s_k points, and then waits for a signal to ar‐
     rive; on return the previous set of masked signals is restored.  The sig‐
     nal mask set is usually empty to indicate that all signals are to be un‐
     blocked for the duration of the call.

     In normal usage, a signal is blocked using sigprocmask(2) to begin a
     critical section, variables modified on the occurrence of the signal are
     examined to determine that there is no work to be done, and the process
     pauses awaiting work by using ssiiggssuussppeenndd() with the previous mask re‐
     turned by sigprocmask(2).

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEESS
     The ssiiggssuussppeenndd() system call always terminates by being interrupted, re‐
     turning -1 with _e_r_r_n_o set to EINTR.

